1
00:00:06,214 --> 00:00:09,217
{\an8}You've had your nose
in that book for the last 10 miles.

2
00:00:09,425 --> 00:00:13,930
What was the name of that redheaded
security guard at the, uh, bioresearch lab?

3
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:15,223
Hardin?

4
00:00:15,932 --> 00:00:18,684
- H-Harken.
- Harken. Harken. Yes, that's it. Thank you.

5
00:00:26,817 --> 00:00:31,614
I hate to be a buzzkill, Jane but even if you
could remember everybody you've ever met

6
00:00:31,822 --> 00:00:33,658
what if Lorelei Martins
is lying?

7
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:36,160
What if you've never
actually met Red John?

8
00:00:36,577 --> 00:00:38,788
I haven't just met him.
I've shaken hands with him.

9
00:00:38,913 --> 00:00:42,375
And she wasn't lying.
She didn't realize what she told me.

10
00:00:44,835 --> 00:00:47,255
- Am I in that book?
- No.

11
00:00:47,922 --> 00:00:52,093
she said it's a wonder
Red John and I didn't become friends.

12
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,762
Now what we have,
I consider a friendship.

13
00:00:55,179 --> 00:00:57,181
So, my friend,
you are free and clear.

14
00:00:57,807 --> 00:00:58,891
That's a relief.

15
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:02,937
- Hey. Are you in charge here?
- Uh, yeah, sure.

16
00:01:03,062 --> 00:01:04,438
Listen, I've been here
for several hours now.

17
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:06,357
I'm a busy man.
When can I leave?

18
00:01:06,899 --> 00:01:09,151
Uh, I don't know.
Who are you?

19
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:12,947
Chip McGavin. I found the body.
I told the officers what I saw.

20
00:01:13,072 --> 00:01:14,865
They said I had to stay here
and say it all over again

21
00:01:14,991 --> 00:01:16,409
to you guys
before I could leave.

22
00:01:17,868 --> 00:01:18,911
Is that so?

23
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,873
- Yes, that's so, Chip.
- This is ridiculous.

24
00:01:22,999 --> 00:01:24,709
Let me tell you something, Chip.
I'll give you a heads-up.

25
00:01:25,126 --> 00:01:26,377
You're our number one
suspect right now

26
00:01:26,502 --> 00:01:28,045
so I'd play it cool
if I were you.

27
00:01:30,464 --> 00:01:31,924
We'll be with you
in a moment.

28
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:33,593
Rigsby.

29
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:38,055
- What have we got?
- You know what? Quit it.

30
00:01:38,681 --> 00:01:42,518
Well, according to his I.D.,
victim's name's Lemuel McVie.

31
00:01:42,685 --> 00:01:44,770
M.E.'s putting time of death
between 10 and 12 hours ago.

32
00:01:45,229 --> 00:01:47,732
Looks like he was hit multiple times
with some kind of blunt object.

33
00:01:48,482 --> 00:01:51,569
Not dressed for golf.
How did he end up in there?

34
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:53,571
Well, we found tire tracks
by the water's edge, so...

35
00:01:53,696 --> 00:01:55,823
which means he was murdered
elsewhere and dumped here.

36
00:01:55,948 --> 00:01:57,908
We need to find
the initial murder site.

37
00:01:58,200 --> 00:01:59,619
His life had just begun.

38
00:02:00,411 --> 00:02:03,706
- He's not that young.
- No, his new life. This is a new wallet.

39
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:07,418
And his driver's license and credit card
were established only two years ago.

40
00:02:07,627 --> 00:02:11,213
- But look at his hands.
- Scarred knuckles.

41
00:02:11,464 --> 00:02:14,216
It's evident that this man
has been in a scrap or two.

42
00:02:15,509 --> 00:02:18,888
- From his old life. Rigsby.
- Yeah. I'm running his license now.

43
00:02:19,513 --> 00:02:21,932
I do like that cufflink.
Very crafty.

44
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:25,561
Perhaps the other one
was lost in the scuffle.

45
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:28,606
Why dump him on a golf course?

46
00:02:28,773 --> 00:02:32,610
Ah, this place goes on for acres and acres.
Multiple points of entry.

47
00:02:33,736 --> 00:02:35,655
I don't think there's
any cameras around here.

48
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:37,323
Seems like the perfect place.

49
00:02:37,531 --> 00:02:41,952
Well, he was busted for marijuana
possession four years ago.

50
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:43,996
And apparently our victim
is associated

51
00:02:44,121 --> 00:02:46,040
with a street gang
called the 10th Street Ghouls.

52
00:02:46,457 --> 00:02:50,127
- That puts a different spin on it.
- Yo, Chip. Front and center.

53
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:52,338
I'm starting to resent you.

54
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:56,592
- Good. You a member of a street gang?
- No, of course not.

55
00:02:56,801 --> 00:02:59,178
- Really? Did you kill this man?
- No, I did not.

56
00:02:59,303 --> 00:03:01,681
Liar. I can see it in your eyes.
Arrest him, Lisbon.

57
00:03:01,889 --> 00:03:04,183
- Wait a minute. You can't do that.
- Yeah, I'm just kidding.

58
00:03:05,601 --> 00:03:09,605
But next time you come across
a dead body, Chip, show a little respect.

59
00:03:10,856 --> 00:03:14,276
- Thank you.
- I'm sorry. Uh, Rigsby...

60
00:03:14,443 --> 00:03:16,737
would you interview Mr. McGavin?
Thank you.

61
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:18,447
All right, Mr. McGavin...

62
00:03:18,572 --> 00:03:20,741
You know what? You can write
my name down in that book.

63
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:22,493
- Oh, come on!
- Put me in it. I want to be in it.

64
00:03:22,618 --> 00:03:24,161
No, no. Come on. Really?

65
00:03:37,848 --> 00:03:40,642
Juliana, we're very sorry
about Lemuel.

66
00:03:41,059 --> 00:03:44,730
My brother's name was Lem.
Nobody called him Lemuel.

67
00:03:45,189 --> 00:03:47,900
Uh, excuse me.
Do you have any tea?

68
00:03:48,692 --> 00:03:52,404
{\an8}- What?
- I'll just take a look in the kitchen.

69
00:03:55,741 --> 00:03:56,784
{\an8}Excuse me?

70
00:03:56,992 --> 00:04:00,162
{\an8}Uh, could...could you show me
where the tea is in the kitchen, please?

71
00:04:08,128 --> 00:04:09,171
{\an8}Thank you.

72
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:15,844
- So how'd they do it?
- Who do you think did it?

73
00:04:16,136 --> 00:04:18,972
{\an8}I don't know.
How did they do it?

74
00:04:21,141 --> 00:04:24,353
{\an8}- He was beaten to death.
- Son of a bitch.

75
00:04:26,104 --> 00:04:28,941
{\an8}Do you think the 10th Street Ghouls
had anything to do with this?

76
00:04:29,066 --> 00:04:31,610
{\an8}No, he's been done with them
almost three years now.

77
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:34,988
{\an8}Is that when Lem got his driver's license
and established those credit cards?

78
00:04:35,614 --> 00:04:38,033
- Yeah.
- You're sure he was out?

79
00:04:38,158 --> 00:04:41,703
{\an8}I'm positive. He jumped out as soon
as they tried to recruit Noah.

80
00:04:41,995 --> 00:04:45,165
{\an8}He wanted to set a better example.
So he got a good job

81
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:48,335
{\an8}and he even helped me
pay for nursing school.

82
00:04:49,169 --> 00:04:51,338
{\an8}Lem stepped up.
He did right by us.

83
00:04:53,215 --> 00:04:57,553
{\an8}Not a single tea drinker in the family.
That's interesting.

84
00:04:57,803 --> 00:04:59,388
{\an8}But you do have cocoa.

85
00:05:01,974 --> 00:05:03,392
You want a cup of hot cocoa?

86
00:05:05,394 --> 00:05:07,688
I'm pretty handy with a kettle.
You should trust me.

87
00:05:10,232 --> 00:05:11,275
{\an8}Nope?

88
00:05:13,694 --> 00:05:17,406
{\an8}- Where was he working?
- Wintergrove Realty.

89
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:20,826
{\an8}- He was a salesman there.
- What about yesterday?

90
00:05:20,993 --> 00:05:24,037
{\an8}Was there anything that he said
or did that jumps out at you?

91
00:05:24,288 --> 00:05:27,749
{\an8}No. Just that he didn't
come home last night.

92
00:05:30,752 --> 00:05:34,339
{\an8}Your parents are gone, aren't they?
You have custody of Noah?

93
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:40,971
{\an8}I know what it's like to be the one to keep
the family together. It's a tough job.

94
00:05:41,972 --> 00:05:43,265
{\an8}Don't make it tougher.

95
00:05:43,974 --> 00:05:46,351
If you know who did this, tell me.

96
00:05:47,728 --> 00:05:49,104
{\an8}I don't know who did this.

97
00:05:50,689 --> 00:05:55,360
It's tough when you lose somebody.
But you still carry them with you.

98
00:05:56,236 --> 00:05:57,821
{\an8}I mean, your memories and...

99
00:05:59,907 --> 00:06:01,408
Choices you make in life.

100
00:06:04,703 --> 00:06:06,705
You made him those
cool cufflinks, didn't you?

101
00:06:07,706 --> 00:06:09,166
{\an8}At school in crafts.

102
00:06:10,334 --> 00:06:12,127
{\an8}I gave them to Lem
for being promoted.

103
00:06:13,170 --> 00:06:15,714
{\an8}He said a man should always dress
like the man he wants to be.

104
00:06:16,882 --> 00:06:21,887
- Sounds like a good brother.
- Yeah.

105
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:25,849
That's a sweet ride
you two have there.

106
00:06:27,601 --> 00:06:30,979
{\an8}Cherry on cherry.
Lem let me choose the stripes.

107
00:06:31,104 --> 00:06:32,147
Great.

108
00:06:32,272 --> 00:06:35,817
It's got a 4-barrel carburetor,
dual exhaust and Nanobrock intakes.

109
00:06:35,943 --> 00:06:39,696
Get out of here.
Nanobrock intakes.

110
00:06:41,573 --> 00:06:42,824
- You ready to go?
- Sure.

111
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:45,285
Yeah. Noah was just telling me
about Lem's car.

112
00:06:47,454 --> 00:06:48,872
Was he driving that yesterday?

113
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:52,626
- You mind if I take a picture?
- No.

114
00:07:01,051 --> 00:07:02,094
Let's go.

115
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:06,306
Keep your chin up.

116
00:07:11,603 --> 00:07:13,855
Here's the picture
of the victim's missing car.

117
00:07:17,150 --> 00:07:19,152
'68 Olds. Nice.

118
00:07:19,528 --> 00:07:21,196
I put a BOLO out
to surrounding agencies.

119
00:07:21,321 --> 00:07:23,573
I'm also checking impound lots
to see if anything came through.

120
00:07:23,699 --> 00:07:25,450
Was there any sign of the car
at the golf course?

121
00:07:25,617 --> 00:07:26,910
He was dumped
from a different car.

122
00:07:27,035 --> 00:07:29,621
The tracks we found by the water were
from a car with a smaller wheelbase.

123
00:07:29,788 --> 00:07:32,749
Lem McVie's car was pretty distinctive.
Maybe he was carjacked.

124
00:07:32,874 --> 00:07:34,668
They would have just left him
in the middle of the street.

125
00:07:34,793 --> 00:07:35,836
This feels more planned.

126
00:07:35,961 --> 00:07:38,171
Dumping the body out in the open
like that feels like a gang hit.

127
00:07:38,338 --> 00:07:40,465
His sister was adamant that
he was done with that life.

128
00:07:40,674 --> 00:07:41,842
Maybe that life
wasn't done with him.

129
00:07:42,384 --> 00:07:44,094
Go upstairs,
talk to somebody in gangs.

130
00:07:44,219 --> 00:07:45,470
See if they can shine
any light on this.

131
00:07:45,595 --> 00:07:49,016
Rigsby, keep checking his cell phone
and his credit card information.

132
00:07:49,891 --> 00:07:51,768
- Van Pelt, stay on the car.
- No problem.

133
00:07:52,352 --> 00:07:55,105
Jane and I are gonna go
to the real estate agency he worked at.

134
00:08:02,779 --> 00:08:04,322
- Agent Wade.
- Come on in.

135
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:10,787
So, what do you have
on Lemuel McVie and the Ghouls?

136
00:08:13,331 --> 00:08:15,584
Looks like your guy's dad
helped start the Ghouls.

137
00:08:16,543 --> 00:08:19,379
Got stabbed while serving
a life sentence in Lompoc.

138
00:08:21,923 --> 00:08:23,008
Lem had some clout.

139
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:26,803
He was selling drugs from at least
a 10-block radius at one point.

140
00:08:27,304 --> 00:08:29,556
Well, you don't get to that kind of position
unless you know how to hustle.

141
00:08:30,098 --> 00:08:34,519
- We heard he jumped out.
- Seems like it.

142
00:08:34,644 --> 00:08:38,231
He hasn't come up in any of our sweeps
for at least a couple years now.

143
00:08:38,398 --> 00:08:40,817
What about gangs that have
a beef with the Ghouls?

144
00:08:41,318 --> 00:08:43,987
- Anybody looking to make a statement?
- No. Nobody.

145
00:08:44,112 --> 00:08:46,031
The Ghouls cleared
everyone out years ago.

146
00:08:47,866 --> 00:08:49,451
Corner shot assault rifle.

147
00:08:50,535 --> 00:08:52,996
I didn't realize gang division
had those kinds of resources.

148
00:08:53,121 --> 00:08:55,165
It's for the new rapid response
team we're creating.

149
00:08:55,415 --> 00:08:58,877
- CBI personnel, SWAT tactics.
- That's a good idea.

150
00:08:59,586 --> 00:09:02,964
- You mind? I haven't seen one in years.
- Go for it.

151
00:09:22,567 --> 00:09:25,529
- You a vet?
- 1st Group, Special Forces. You?

152
00:09:25,654 --> 00:09:29,866
Marines. Better put that thing down
before you shoot someone, doggie.

153
00:09:40,544 --> 00:09:44,089
- Thank you.
- Anytime.

154
00:09:49,553 --> 00:09:51,388
{\an8}Mr. Dilmer's waiting for you
in his office.

155
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:54,099
Any interest in doing
a tour of the model homes

156
00:09:54,224 --> 00:09:56,351
- after your business is done?
- Are you serious?

157
00:09:56,685 --> 00:09:59,104
Well, interest rates couldn't be lower.
It's a great time to buy.

158
00:09:59,396 --> 00:10:01,815
- Pass.
- I'd like to take a look.

159
00:10:01,982 --> 00:10:03,942
- You can count me in, Tiffany.
- Great.

160
00:10:05,819 --> 00:10:06,862
Mr. Dilmer?

161
00:10:08,238 --> 00:10:11,158
This is Agent Lisbon from the CBI.
And this is Mr. Jane.

162
00:10:11,283 --> 00:10:15,078
Oh, of course. Nathan Dilmer.
Thanks, Tiffany.

163
00:10:15,537 --> 00:10:16,913
See you later, Mr. Jane.

164
00:10:18,081 --> 00:10:21,334
Uh, before you go, did either of you
see Lem leave last night?

165
00:10:21,501 --> 00:10:24,296
I did. I was heading out
for a mixer

166
00:10:24,421 --> 00:10:26,006
and I saw him leave
at the end of the day.

167
00:10:26,131 --> 00:10:27,716
It, uh, was about 6:30.

168
00:10:27,841 --> 00:10:29,426
- In his car?
- Yes.

169
00:10:29,551 --> 00:10:32,679
Yeah, because when I left around
a little after 7:00 his car was gone.

170
00:10:32,804 --> 00:10:34,055
Well, thank you, Tiffany.

171
00:10:36,933 --> 00:10:40,437
What a tragedy.
I worry about how everyone's coping.

172
00:10:40,896 --> 00:10:43,648
Oh, they're just anxious about
who's gonna get Lem's leads.

173
00:10:44,524 --> 00:10:47,027
That's not how I run my office.

174
00:10:47,903 --> 00:10:52,157
I've worked hard to foster
a cohesive and dynamic community here.

175
00:10:52,324 --> 00:10:55,160
We just got back from our annual
office retreat, in fact.

176
00:10:55,410 --> 00:10:56,995
Lem was family.

177
00:10:58,246 --> 00:10:59,915
Hey. Put another one
on the board for me, Dil.

178
00:11:00,123 --> 00:11:03,376
Oh, yeah. Victor, uh, this is
Agent Lisbon from the CBI.

179
00:11:03,501 --> 00:11:07,923
This is her associate Mr. Jane.
Victor Phipps, my top man.

180
00:11:08,757 --> 00:11:11,092
Uh, poor Lem.
Tragedy. Tragedy.

181
00:11:12,135 --> 00:11:14,137
That champagne must have
a bitter taste to it.

182
00:11:15,388 --> 00:11:17,140
I don't want to argue, Detective.

183
00:11:19,184 --> 00:11:21,353
But it's just a sales prop.
Show must go on.

184
00:11:21,603 --> 00:11:25,607
Victor was Lem's supervisor.
They made an outstanding team.

185
00:11:25,941 --> 00:11:27,609
He was a good kid.
Good salesman.

186
00:11:28,109 --> 00:11:30,779
You saw something in Lem
that you recognized.

187
00:11:30,904 --> 00:11:31,947
Sure.

188
00:11:32,072 --> 00:11:34,741
Uh, because you both had a different
kind of life before real estate

189
00:11:34,866 --> 00:11:37,077
- different kind of jobs?
- That's right.

190
00:11:37,285 --> 00:11:42,123
- You're a... Disbarred lawyer.
- You reading my mail?

191
00:11:43,124 --> 00:11:48,088
No. But you just said
"nolo contendere." Lawyer.

192
00:11:48,213 --> 00:11:51,383
And you wouldn't be working here
if you weren't disbarred. No offense.

193
00:11:52,342 --> 00:11:56,763
Corporate litigation. 14 years. Partner
the last six. I took the rap for a client.

194
00:11:58,014 --> 00:12:02,269
What we were is not who we are.
That's my motto.

195
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:04,771
We're big on giving people
second chances around here.

196
00:12:04,896 --> 00:12:07,232
So you knew about Lem's
gang situation then?

197
00:12:07,357 --> 00:12:09,901
Well, he came to us through
a program for ex-gang youth.

198
00:12:10,068 --> 00:12:13,530
An office full of second chancers.
And you're the head of the office.

199
00:12:13,655 --> 00:12:15,991
So are you a Saint
or another reformed sinner?

200
00:12:16,283 --> 00:12:17,534
Aren't we here
to talk about Lem?

201
00:12:17,867 --> 00:12:21,079
Well, you strike me as
the consensus-building type

202
00:12:21,204 --> 00:12:25,083
so I'm gonna guess
that your crime was fraud.

203
00:12:26,293 --> 00:12:27,919
Ponzi scheme, perhaps.

204
00:12:29,170 --> 00:12:31,673
I paid back society
for the errors in my judgment.

205
00:12:33,049 --> 00:12:36,594
My mistakes do not define me
moving forward.

206
00:12:37,387 --> 00:12:38,430
Another one.

207
00:12:39,848 --> 00:12:42,142
Can either of you think of anybody
that Lem may have had a problem

208
00:12:42,267 --> 00:12:44,644
with like a coworker, client?

209
00:12:45,145 --> 00:12:49,316
Actually, he did come to work,
uh, a bit banged up... about a month ago?

210
00:12:49,816 --> 00:12:54,362
- I'm sorry. "Banged up"?
- Uh, black eye. Uh, tried to cover it up.

211
00:12:54,487 --> 00:12:57,115
I asked him if he needed any help.
He said that he could handle it.

212
00:12:59,492 --> 00:13:00,535
What do you got?

213
00:13:00,869 --> 00:13:03,371
I got back the preliminary
autopsy report.

214
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:05,665
Lem was struck with
several different objects.

215
00:13:05,790 --> 00:13:08,209
But the X-rays showed
some older fractures.

216
00:13:08,335 --> 00:13:10,045
One to his ribs couldn't have
been more than a month old.

217
00:13:11,004 --> 00:13:14,049
His boss said he came into work
pretty banged up around a month ago.

218
00:13:14,299 --> 00:13:15,759
Based on that,
I did some digging.

219
00:13:16,176 --> 00:13:19,846
Lem visited the emergency room three times
in the last year for various injuries...

220
00:13:20,096 --> 00:13:21,306
all listed as accidents.

221
00:13:21,598 --> 00:13:23,850
It says here his sister
Juliana signed him out.

222
00:13:24,017 --> 00:13:27,312
- She signed him out every time.
- Why didn't she tell us that?

223
00:13:30,523 --> 00:13:33,109
You don't know where Juliana is
and you haven't heard from her?

224
00:13:33,401 --> 00:13:34,861
She's just out.

225
00:13:38,656 --> 00:13:40,492
You don't have anything else
you want to tell us?

226
00:13:40,742 --> 00:13:41,785
Nothing.

227
00:13:41,993 --> 00:13:43,870
You know, the way
you're clenching your fists

228
00:13:43,995 --> 00:13:46,289
suggests that you do have
something you want to tell us.

229
00:13:49,793 --> 00:13:53,171
We can't talk to you.
Y'all can't do anything.

230
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:57,092
I know Lem would never want you to be afraid
to look another man in the eye, right?

231
00:13:58,385 --> 00:14:03,556
He told me to stay strong,
no matter what happens.

232
00:14:03,681 --> 00:14:06,684
You are being strong, but,
you know your sister needs help.

233
00:14:08,395 --> 00:14:09,479
Where'd she go?

234
00:14:18,696 --> 00:14:24,869
- Hey! Where's Shade?
- Man...

235
00:14:24,994 --> 00:14:27,163
- Where is he?!
- Is she serious?

236
00:14:27,288 --> 00:14:29,332
Yo, yo, you better get up out of here
with all that, little girl.

237
00:14:29,499 --> 00:14:32,168
You see where you at, right?
Go home, man.

238
00:14:32,585 --> 00:14:33,628
Let's go, guys.

239
00:14:33,795 --> 00:14:36,423
I said, where is Shade?

240
00:14:39,467 --> 00:14:40,802
You think it's funny?

241
00:14:44,347 --> 00:14:47,308
- Police! Drop the weapon now!
- What?

242
00:14:48,768 --> 00:14:50,895
Drop your weapon now!

243
00:14:52,313 --> 00:14:53,690
Put your hands up!

244
00:14:55,233 --> 00:14:57,277
Police, drop your weapon!
Everybody, hands in the air!

245
00:14:57,402 --> 00:14:59,195
Don't move, stay where you are!

246
00:14:59,529 --> 00:15:04,534
- Juliana, put the gun down.
- No!

247
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:08,830
They were hanging out laughing.
They just killed my brother!

248
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:10,582
Like it was nothing!

249
00:15:12,417 --> 00:15:15,086
Put it down
so we don't have to use ours.

250
00:15:20,300 --> 00:15:26,347
Juliana... Please.
Put it down and let's talk.

251
00:15:33,146 --> 00:15:34,230
Get away.

252
00:15:38,318 --> 00:15:40,069
Up against the fence.
Go!

253
00:15:41,279 --> 00:15:42,322
Hands up.

254
00:15:57,826 --> 00:16:00,579
Where's Noah? He's never
been alone all night.

255
00:16:00,829 --> 00:16:03,332
Sit down.
Noah's fine.

256
00:16:03,666 --> 00:16:05,209
He spent the night
with your neighbor Yasmin

257
00:16:05,334 --> 00:16:06,961
and we brought him here
this morning.

258
00:16:09,546 --> 00:16:14,259
- Where did you get the gun?
- Lem had it hidden for protection.

259
00:16:14,635 --> 00:16:18,097
You think the Ghouls are behind
Lem's murder. Anyone in particular?

260
00:16:18,555 --> 00:16:19,974
Yeah, this dude named Shade.

261
00:16:20,516 --> 00:16:22,434
Lem used to work corners
for him back in the day.

262
00:16:23,185 --> 00:16:25,562
Is that who fractured
Lem's rib earlier this year?

263
00:16:26,522 --> 00:16:28,315
Shade was hella pissed
when Lem quit.

264
00:16:29,358 --> 00:16:32,027
But he had to take it.
Lem jumped out.

265
00:16:32,903 --> 00:16:35,531
Then he just started going in
hard on Lem this year.

266
00:16:35,823 --> 00:16:36,865
Why now?

267
00:16:36,991 --> 00:16:40,202
Well, Shade wasn't making enough money
selling drugs as he was with Lem

268
00:16:40,327 --> 00:16:44,206
- so he felt like Lem owed him.
- And Lem just let him do it?

269
00:16:44,748 --> 00:16:47,209
Yeah, Lem said eventually
Shade would move on.

270
00:16:48,002 --> 00:16:49,503
And I thought he was right.

271
00:16:50,713 --> 00:16:56,593
- And then Shade came back?
- Yeah. The night Lem died.

272
00:16:57,302 --> 00:17:00,389
Shade came to the house
banging on the door like 8:30

273
00:17:00,723 --> 00:17:03,183
mouthing off about how
Lem was gonna get his.

274
00:17:04,309 --> 00:17:06,437
And I called Lem,
but he never answered his phone.

275
00:17:06,979 --> 00:17:10,774
- Juliana, you should have called the police.
- And told them what?

276
00:17:11,150 --> 00:17:14,403
- That a Ghoul was looking for an ex-Ghoul?
- They could have helped you.

277
00:17:14,653 --> 00:17:17,531
Lady, my mother was shot
in broad daylight.

278
00:17:18,449 --> 00:17:20,659
Three years later, they still
don't know who did it.

279
00:17:21,994 --> 00:17:26,123
Cops? Please. Y'all just show up
to collect the bodies.

280
00:17:27,207 --> 00:17:29,084
Are we done?
I need to get my brother home.

281
00:17:29,626 --> 00:17:33,922
Juliana, you discharged
an unregistered firearm.

282
00:17:34,465 --> 00:17:39,136
- You're gonna be arrested.
- Arrested? Well, what about Noah?

283
00:17:40,179 --> 00:17:42,014
You're not gonna put him
in foster care, are you?

284
00:17:43,098 --> 00:17:47,478
If there's no immediate family that
can take custody, that's what happens.

285
00:17:48,812 --> 00:17:49,897
I'm sorry.

286
00:17:52,316 --> 00:17:55,027
You... You can't do this!

287
00:17:56,445 --> 00:17:57,488
Hey, boss.

288
00:17:57,738 --> 00:17:59,865
Shade's real name...
Jeron Slaughter.

289
00:18:01,075 --> 00:18:04,870
Receiving stolen goods,
armed robbery, and attempted murder.

290
00:18:04,995 --> 00:18:06,038
He's a real charmer.

291
00:18:06,163 --> 00:18:08,373
Well, he's in charge of enforcing
discipline within the Ghouls.

292
00:18:08,665 --> 00:18:10,292
Beats down crew members
when they step out of line.

293
00:18:10,501 --> 00:18:12,044
- Do we have a location on Shade?
- Maybe.

294
00:18:12,169 --> 00:18:14,004
He hangs at one of their stash
houses several times a week.

295
00:18:14,338 --> 00:18:15,380
All right, go and pick him up.

296
00:18:15,506 --> 00:18:18,258
Well, the stash house is fortified...
barred windows, steel doors.

297
00:18:18,634 --> 00:18:20,385
Maybe we could use the new
rapid response team.

298
00:18:20,511 --> 00:18:22,596
Coordinate it.
I need to call Child Services.

299
00:18:22,721 --> 00:18:23,764
You got it.

300
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:34,566
Hey.

301
00:18:37,653 --> 00:18:41,115
Nobody's telling me what's going on.
Do you know where my sister is?

302
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:44,618
Yeah. She's... she's just
down the hall. She's okay.

303
00:18:47,538 --> 00:18:50,833
- How are you doing?
- I'm okay.

304
00:18:54,920 --> 00:18:56,380
Hi, Noah.

305
00:18:58,465 --> 00:19:01,468
- Listen. We have a situation...
- you know, Lisbon, I-I have a great idea.

306
00:19:01,885 --> 00:19:04,221
A brilliant idea. Excuse me.
One second. Come here.

307
00:19:09,434 --> 00:19:11,019
What brilliant idea?

308
00:19:13,897 --> 00:19:15,149
Well, um...

309
00:19:16,024 --> 00:19:17,067
There isn't one.

310
00:19:18,694 --> 00:19:20,988
I just don't want you to send
that kid to a foster home.

311
00:19:21,363 --> 00:19:23,907
- I don't have a choice.
- Well, there's always choices.

312
00:19:24,408 --> 00:19:27,870
- Okay, like what?
- Let 'em go home.

313
00:19:28,829 --> 00:19:30,372
You don't have to charge
his sister yet, do you?

314
00:19:30,497 --> 00:19:33,542
Just... Just... just think
about it for a while.

315
00:19:34,126 --> 00:19:38,297
She broke the law. I already called
the D.A. and Child Services.

316
00:19:38,505 --> 00:19:39,715
Just let 'em go home.

317
00:19:50,517 --> 00:19:51,935
Got Lem's phone records.

318
00:19:52,186 --> 00:19:54,021
He had two calls
from a disposable phone

319
00:19:54,146 --> 00:19:56,481
one at 8:15 p.m.
and one at 9:00 p.m.

320
00:19:56,607 --> 00:19:58,442
The call at 9:00
is within our murder window.

321
00:19:58,734 --> 00:19:59,776
Well, that must be our killer.

322
00:19:59,902 --> 00:20:02,112
Try and track down where
the cell phone was sold.

323
00:20:02,529 --> 00:20:05,157
Hey, Rigsby, did they ever find that
other cufflink at the country club?

324
00:20:05,282 --> 00:20:06,325
No. They went through
the water hazard

325
00:20:06,450 --> 00:20:08,243
with a vacuum and a sifter...
came back with nothing.

326
00:20:10,746 --> 00:20:12,789
- Where you goin'?
- Back to Wintergrove.

327
00:20:12,915 --> 00:20:15,167
I have an appointment
to take a look at a model home.

328
00:20:15,292 --> 00:20:18,170
Do you want to come with or are you
gonna stick around and do the right thing?

329
00:20:19,463 --> 00:20:20,505
Jane.

330
00:20:21,340 --> 00:20:23,425
I trust you'll make
the right choice, Lisbon.

331
00:20:27,137 --> 00:20:28,263
Damn you, Jane.

332
00:20:29,514 --> 00:20:32,100
Tell Juliana McVie
she's free to go for now.

333
00:20:32,267 --> 00:20:34,561
Tell her we'll be in touch
about the charges against her.

334
00:20:34,728 --> 00:20:37,522
- Really? But we...
- I know, I know. Just do it, huh?

335
00:20:37,898 --> 00:20:40,400
I gotta go call off
Child Services and the D.A.

336
00:20:48,909 --> 00:20:52,704
Wintergrove in the Woods.
Is this a... company retreat?

337
00:20:52,829 --> 00:20:56,250
- Did you...did you go?
- Up in Carrington? No.

338
00:20:56,458 --> 00:20:58,168
That's strictly for the top salesmen.

339
00:20:59,753 --> 00:21:01,046
I see Lem was there.

340
00:21:01,797 --> 00:21:04,716
Yeah. He was always running
behind the big dogs.

341
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:05,884
"Big dogs"?

342
00:21:06,426 --> 00:21:09,429
The top salesmen in our company...
Dilmer, Phipps, and Bosh.

343
00:21:14,935 --> 00:21:17,437
- Are you ready for the tour?
- Can't wait.

344
00:21:21,441 --> 00:21:23,235
Tiffany, we, uh... we doing a tour?

345
00:21:23,402 --> 00:21:26,863
Yes. I was just about to take
Mr. Jane to see the models.

346
00:21:27,281 --> 00:21:29,574
She's got pluck, this one.
Honey, I'll take it from here.

347
00:21:29,908 --> 00:21:31,410
Why don't you input
the day's contracts?

348
00:21:36,290 --> 00:21:41,795
- Of course, Mr. Bosh.
- Mr. Jane. Quinton Bosh.

349
00:21:42,129 --> 00:21:46,466
- Bosh. Ah, you're one of the big dogs.
- Well, that's what they tell me.

350
00:21:46,967 --> 00:21:48,802
So, Mr. Jane, what are you
looking for in a home?

351
00:21:50,178 --> 00:21:52,139
Wh-what's that place over there?

352
00:21:53,015 --> 00:21:55,225
- Oh, the Catalina.
- Yeah. That looks interesting.

353
00:21:55,934 --> 00:21:58,353
Well...
I need, uh, large square footage.

354
00:21:58,478 --> 00:22:03,275
4-bedroom, 3 1/2 bathrooms
the place oozes with charm.

355
00:22:07,154 --> 00:22:10,032
Shade's been in there about 40 minutes
so he's nice and tucked in.

356
00:22:10,282 --> 00:22:12,659
"Alpha to Wade.
Three just entered the side of the house."

357
00:22:13,076 --> 00:22:14,995
Copy.
More at the party.

358
00:22:15,620 --> 00:22:17,706
Because of the fortification,
I'm thinking the front door

359
00:22:17,831 --> 00:22:18,874
is the best point of entry.

360
00:22:18,999 --> 00:22:21,084
Cho, you take the two side.
Once we do our thing

361
00:22:21,209 --> 00:22:22,544
- we'll hand him to you.
- Copy that.

362
00:22:23,628 --> 00:22:25,505
Okay, ladies, time to move.

363
00:22:25,964 --> 00:22:27,049
Let's go.

364
00:22:30,677 --> 00:22:31,720
Nickel.

365
00:22:37,184 --> 00:22:40,103
- Yo, get out man!
- CBI! Hands in the air!

366
00:22:41,188 --> 00:22:42,939
Cho, we got a runner.
He's headed your way.

367
00:22:46,818 --> 00:22:47,861
I got him.

368
00:23:01,541 --> 00:23:02,793
Give me your arm.

369
00:23:07,172 --> 00:23:08,215
Get up.

370
00:23:13,178 --> 00:23:16,306
Hey.
You found our victim's car. Well done.

371
00:23:33,532 --> 00:23:36,618
Now, the living room
is the centerpiece

372
00:23:36,743 --> 00:23:39,162
of the Catalina's
4,000-square-foot living area.

373
00:23:39,538 --> 00:23:44,209
And over here we have slab granite
countertops in your gourmet kitchen.

374
00:23:53,635 --> 00:23:55,887
Nice flooring. Very solid.

375
00:23:56,096 --> 00:23:58,723
Yeah. Nothing but upscale
to the max in a Wintergrove home.

376
00:24:01,309 --> 00:24:02,602
Not really feeling it.

377
00:24:02,727 --> 00:24:05,439
You know, I gotta...
I gotta... feel it.

378
00:24:06,064 --> 00:24:08,400
Let's go take a gander
at the other model, shall we?

379
00:24:08,567 --> 00:24:09,651
- The Catalina II?
- Yeah.

380
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:13,238
Well, it's the exact same layout except
for the extra bedroom and decor theme.

381
00:24:13,363 --> 00:24:16,992
- Sounds good.
- Right.

382
00:24:27,294 --> 00:24:31,756
So this is Catalina II. It's a whopping
4,300 square feet of living space.

383
00:24:32,716 --> 00:24:36,803
- Um, Mr. Jane. Can I help you?
- Uh, Mr. Jane is with me, Phippsy. I got it.

384
00:24:37,929 --> 00:24:40,599
Sure, Bosh.
Um, let me show you the garage.

385
00:24:41,141 --> 00:24:43,560
Ample space for three cars.

386
00:24:45,479 --> 00:24:47,898
Well, as I said living room
is exactly the same.

387
00:24:48,398 --> 00:24:51,693
Well, not exactly. I like it.

388
00:24:52,277 --> 00:24:53,904
Good. Good. Let's take
a look at the extra bedroom.

389
00:24:54,029 --> 00:24:55,071
No. No need.

390
00:24:56,281 --> 00:24:58,450
I'm really feeling it here.
Yeah.

391
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:01,036
I think I need to be alone
with the house for a moment.

392
00:25:01,328 --> 00:25:03,997
You know, just to check out its aura.

393
00:25:05,290 --> 00:25:07,000
- Aura?
- Yeah.

394
00:25:08,001 --> 00:25:11,630
Well, I'm not gonna buy a house
unless we have compatible auras.

395
00:25:13,798 --> 00:25:16,176
- Of course.
- Ten minutes.

396
00:25:17,511 --> 00:25:18,970
Good. I'll, uh...
I'll be right outside.

397
00:25:21,556 --> 00:25:22,682
Thank you.

398
00:25:39,032 --> 00:25:41,076
- Lisbon?
- "What are you doing?"

399
00:25:41,618 --> 00:25:42,744
Following a hunch.

400
00:25:43,286 --> 00:25:45,914
Uh, you've been following it
for several hours now.

401
00:25:46,122 --> 00:25:48,375
Yeah, well, some take
longer than others.

402
00:25:48,625 --> 00:25:51,878
Playtime's over.
We found Lem's missing car.

403
00:25:52,629 --> 00:25:58,927
Ah. That's excellent. Because I just found
the room where he was murdered.

404
00:26:12,989 --> 00:26:14,073
"Shade."

405
00:26:15,408 --> 00:26:17,326
Tell me how you ended up
with a dead man's car.

406
00:26:19,787 --> 00:26:21,831
- To settle a debt.
- A debt?

407
00:26:23,332 --> 00:26:25,710
Well, he cost me a lot of money
when he jumped out.

408
00:26:26,669 --> 00:26:27,795
I mean, I was cool.

409
00:26:28,546 --> 00:26:31,090
But he started flexing with
a very nice ride this year.

410
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:34,010
See he obviously forgot
that I made him.

411
00:26:34,844 --> 00:26:36,137
So I'm like, "yo"

412
00:26:36,596 --> 00:26:38,431
"I'm-a need a little more
return on my investment."

413
00:26:38,598 --> 00:26:40,099
Now is this when you
bruised his sternum

414
00:26:40,516 --> 00:26:42,226
or are you talking about
when you fractured his ribs?

415
00:26:42,977 --> 00:26:46,439
"You're funny.
Let's just say he agreed with me."

416
00:26:47,315 --> 00:26:48,608
Up until two nights ago.

417
00:26:48,774 --> 00:26:51,027
Look, I just called to tell him
that I need a little extra.

418
00:26:51,277 --> 00:26:53,821
See, these corners ain't doing
what they should in this economy.

419
00:26:54,113 --> 00:26:56,699
At 8:15 and 9:00 p.m.
on a disposable phone?

420
00:26:56,866 --> 00:26:58,367
Hey, it's cheaper than a plan, man.

421
00:27:00,578 --> 00:27:04,624
Anyway, he tells me go to hell.
He ain't coming off no more cheddar.

422
00:27:04,749 --> 00:27:06,083
And we know you went
to his house to find him.

423
00:27:06,208 --> 00:27:07,585
He wasn't there.
Where'd you go?

424
00:27:09,670 --> 00:27:12,214
- His job.
- You found him at work?

425
00:27:12,632 --> 00:27:15,259
No, his car was parked out in front
of them fake houses so I took it.

426
00:27:15,801 --> 00:27:18,262
And we found evidence that he was killed
in one of those model homes

427
00:27:18,387 --> 00:27:20,097
around the same time
you stole his car.

428
00:27:20,514 --> 00:27:22,058
Now why should I believe
you didn't kill him?

429
00:27:22,308 --> 00:27:24,060
Because that wouldn't be necessary.

430
00:27:24,852 --> 00:27:28,314
See, once I got the car,
I was paid in full.

431
00:27:30,149 --> 00:27:33,611
So Shade stole Lem's car but why does
my gut say he's not the killer?

432
00:27:33,861 --> 00:27:35,237
Well, you have a very smart gut.

433
00:27:35,363 --> 00:27:37,323
And the one thing that
Shade's story does confirm

434
00:27:37,448 --> 00:27:39,617
is that Lem was over at
the model homes after 8:00 p.m.

435
00:27:39,742 --> 00:27:41,619
Long after his coworkers
said he'd left.

436
00:27:41,744 --> 00:27:43,871
Trouble is, anyone can
get into those model homes.

437
00:27:43,996 --> 00:27:45,831
- But what's the motive?
- Still remains to be seen.

438
00:27:45,957 --> 00:27:48,209
I'll put in a request
for CSI at Catalina II.

439
00:27:48,542 --> 00:27:50,711
Hey, boss. I finished
going through Lem's computer.

440
00:27:50,836 --> 00:27:53,881
He recently set up news alerts
for the Carrington Gazette.

441
00:27:54,507 --> 00:27:55,549
Carrington?

442
00:27:55,675 --> 00:27:57,385
Yeah, it's a little town where
they have their company retreat.

443
00:27:58,219 --> 00:28:02,723
According to the browser history
he recently read alerts about burglaries

444
00:28:02,932 --> 00:28:04,976
a missing hunter,
and a house fire?

445
00:28:05,685 --> 00:28:06,727
That's interesting.

446
00:28:06,852 --> 00:28:09,397
Uh, why set up a filter
for a podunk newspaper?

447
00:28:11,524 --> 00:28:14,193
I need Lem's car.
Hold off on that CSI team.

448
00:28:14,318 --> 00:28:16,445
Let's keep the murder site
under wraps for now.

449
00:28:16,654 --> 00:28:17,822
What? Why?

450
00:28:48,436 --> 00:28:51,147
- This is his car, right?
- Definitely.

451
00:28:51,981 --> 00:28:53,065
How'd it get here?

452
00:28:53,357 --> 00:28:55,067
- Should I call the police?
- Yes.

453
00:28:56,485 --> 00:28:57,778
And you better call Dilmer.

454
00:29:09,707 --> 00:29:11,417
- Hey, Sarah.
- Hey.

455
00:29:11,667 --> 00:29:14,086
- How's life as an A.D.A.?
- Ah, I'm enjoying it.

456
00:29:14,420 --> 00:29:16,589
Look, I tried to reach you
before you came down.

457
00:29:16,839 --> 00:29:19,341
We'd like to hold off
on charging Juliana McVie.

458
00:29:19,467 --> 00:29:21,218
- Just a beat.
- Why? It sounds like a dunk.

459
00:29:21,469 --> 00:29:23,220
Ah, this case is still developing

460
00:29:23,429 --> 00:29:26,390
and, you know, we're hoping
to use her against some bigger fish.

461
00:29:26,515 --> 00:29:30,102
- I'd like to have the leverage.
- Okay. But I've read the file.

462
00:29:30,269 --> 00:29:31,812
Don't give her a pass
because she's sympathetic.

463
00:29:31,937 --> 00:29:34,148
She broke the law.
You have statutory duties.

464
00:29:34,857 --> 00:29:38,861
- I hear you. How's Ben?
- He's so wonderful.

465
00:29:39,195 --> 00:29:40,738
Hey, keep me apprised
on McVie.

466
00:29:43,657 --> 00:29:45,910
It was parked here
when we opened the office.

467
00:29:46,077 --> 00:29:47,828
Note was already
on the windshield.

468
00:29:48,996 --> 00:29:52,208
"Bring $50,000 to the place
where you killed Lemuel McVie"

469
00:29:52,333 --> 00:29:55,294
"at 10:30 tonight
or I go to the police at 11:00."

470
00:29:55,711 --> 00:29:57,046
It doesn't make any sense.

471
00:29:57,171 --> 00:29:59,090
This car was stolen
by the killer, wasn't it?

472
00:29:59,215 --> 00:30:01,550
- That's our supposition.
- So what does this mean?

473
00:30:02,426 --> 00:30:05,513
I don't know, Mr. Dilmer.
Someone's playing games, I guess.

474
00:30:05,721 --> 00:30:07,139
Or someone here knows more
than they're telling.

475
00:30:07,348 --> 00:30:08,641
You guys don't have a clue.

476
00:30:08,974 --> 00:30:11,393
I mean, have you even figured
out where Lem was murdered yet?

477
00:30:11,936 --> 00:30:13,312
Thank you for your assistance,
ladies and gentlemen.

478
00:30:13,437 --> 00:30:15,022
You can head on back
to work now.

479
00:30:15,314 --> 00:30:17,983
Like I thought.
You don't know anything.

480
00:30:26,992 --> 00:30:29,161
Okay.
The hook has been baited.

481
00:30:33,499 --> 00:30:36,585
- So now we're breaking and entering?
- Uh, no, 'cause, uh...

482
00:30:37,253 --> 00:30:39,463
I have the key.
Come on.

483
00:30:45,219 --> 00:30:48,639
- It was locked this morning.
- Looked easy.

484
00:30:55,938 --> 00:31:00,025
- No, it's just theft and illegal entry.
- Ladies first.

485
00:31:03,821 --> 00:31:07,658
What makes you so certain
that the killer works at Wintergrove Realty?

486
00:31:07,867 --> 00:31:12,788
Imagine, Lisbon... for a mere 20% down,
all this could be yours.

487
00:31:13,455 --> 00:31:16,041
Fully furnished, lavishly equipped.

488
00:31:16,208 --> 00:31:21,046
- Lacking only one thing.
- What?

489
00:31:23,090 --> 00:31:27,428
Well, isn't it obvious that there should be
a lovely, decorative rug right here

490
00:31:27,553 --> 00:31:29,138
pulling the whole room together?

491
00:31:29,513 --> 00:31:31,348
I mean, the other model had one.

492
00:31:32,224 --> 00:31:34,185
So that's what the killer
used to soak up the blood

493
00:31:34,310 --> 00:31:36,687
- and get the body out of here?
- Precisely.

494
00:31:37,062 --> 00:31:38,272
We should wait in here.

495
00:31:39,315 --> 00:31:40,941
Oh, here. Do you want a drink?

496
00:31:42,401 --> 00:31:45,738
That still doesn't prove that the killer
is a Wintergrove employee.

497
00:31:46,071 --> 00:31:49,575
Well, if it wasn't, why wouldn't they
just leave the body here?

498
00:31:49,783 --> 00:31:51,285
Uh, what's the motive?

499
00:31:51,702 --> 00:31:53,370
Something happened
at the company retreat

500
00:31:53,495 --> 00:31:54,663
- something that...
- What?

501
00:31:54,788 --> 00:31:57,333
Well, we will ask whoever
comes through that front door.

502
00:31:57,833 --> 00:31:59,210
- Down.
- Really?

503
00:31:59,627 --> 00:32:00,711
Come on.

504
00:32:04,215 --> 00:32:05,549
- Hi.
- Hi.

505
00:32:22,441 --> 00:32:23,484
What?

506
00:32:23,859 --> 00:32:26,111
It's okay.
You were talking in your sleep.

507
00:32:26,612 --> 00:32:29,281
- I was?
- And drooling a little.

508
00:32:30,074 --> 00:32:33,661
- W-well, what did I say?
- Well...someone's coming.

509
00:33:03,630 --> 00:33:05,340
- Bosh.
- What?

510
00:33:05,465 --> 00:33:06,966
- That doesn't make sense.
- Why?

511
00:33:07,634 --> 00:33:08,760
He's lazy.

512
00:33:12,430 --> 00:33:14,057
- Hey.
- Hey.

513
00:33:16,601 --> 00:33:19,771
Traffic's crazy out there.
Didn't think I'd make it in time.

514
00:33:23,650 --> 00:33:27,028
Phipps?
He and Bosh hate each other.

515
00:33:33,451 --> 00:33:38,164
- Gentlemen.
- Did you get it?

516
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:41,918
Yeah, took some doing.
I got it all.

517
00:33:42,251 --> 00:33:45,755
- Cash is in the bag.
- Then I guess we just wait.

518
00:33:46,381 --> 00:33:49,592
- Dilmer?
- The consensus builder.

519
00:33:50,510 --> 00:33:52,512
I don't like this.
I got a bad feeling about this.

520
00:33:52,637 --> 00:33:53,763
Stop your whimpering.

521
00:33:53,888 --> 00:33:54,931
- You got a better idea?
- Screw you!

522
00:33:55,056 --> 00:33:56,099
- Hey, knock it off.
- Screw you, I...

523
00:33:56,224 --> 00:33:58,017
knock it off! Both of you.

524
00:33:58,142 --> 00:34:00,645
We gotta stick together here.
No more squabbling.

525
00:34:01,229 --> 00:34:02,480
Evening, gentlemen.

526
00:34:04,774 --> 00:34:07,527
- What are you doing here?
- Catching killers.

527
00:34:09,278 --> 00:34:12,532
- But the letter...
- He wrote the letter, you idiot!

528
00:34:12,657 --> 00:34:14,951
Ah, you're a quick study there, Phipps.

529
00:34:15,910 --> 00:34:17,662
Quick enough to know
there isn't a law in the land

530
00:34:17,787 --> 00:34:19,122
that's gonna convict us of murder

531
00:34:19,247 --> 00:34:21,916
based off of our compliance
with a vague letter.

532
00:34:23,042 --> 00:34:24,836
That is not entirely true.

533
00:34:24,961 --> 00:34:27,505
- We have motive.
- How do you figure?

534
00:34:27,755 --> 00:34:29,716
You know Lem started reading
the Carrington Gazette

535
00:34:29,841 --> 00:34:31,217
when he came back
from the retreat?

536
00:34:31,551 --> 00:34:35,513
He was particularly interested
in a story about a missing hunter.

537
00:34:36,431 --> 00:34:38,766
But the hunter wasn't
just missing, was he?

538
00:34:39,017 --> 00:34:40,101
He was dead.

539
00:34:41,185 --> 00:34:43,563
- We don't know anything about a hunter.
- Okay.

540
00:34:44,564 --> 00:34:45,898
Yeah. Thanks, Bosh.

541
00:34:47,108 --> 00:34:50,778
- You can go. We'll be in touch.
- What?

542
00:34:51,738 --> 00:34:54,407
Thank you for your assistance.
We can handle this.

543
00:34:54,699 --> 00:34:57,368
Wait. Wait a minute.
You cut a deal?

544
00:34:57,493 --> 00:34:58,703
No, no, no, of course...
I don't know...

545
00:34:58,828 --> 00:34:59,871
I have no idea what
they're talking about.

546
00:34:59,996 --> 00:35:01,372
Then tell me why you get to go!

547
00:35:01,497 --> 00:35:04,500
You really think Bosh is the kind of guy
that's gonna sit around and take the rap

548
00:35:04,625 --> 00:35:06,127
when you two dopes are available?

549
00:35:07,962 --> 00:35:09,881
- Tell me you didn't sell us out.
- No, no, he's messing with you!

550
00:35:10,006 --> 00:35:11,049
Then what did you say?!
What did you say?!

551
00:35:11,174 --> 00:35:14,761
- He's messing with you, jackass!
- Correct.

552
00:35:17,805 --> 00:35:21,851
I was just messing with you.
Lisbon, arrest these clowns.

553
00:35:22,477 --> 00:35:24,395
Put your hands
on your heads now!

554
00:35:51,172 --> 00:35:54,509
The wheelbase and tire pattern of your S.U.V.
match the tracks at the golf course.

555
00:35:55,676 --> 00:35:58,513
- Congratulations.
- Tell me what happened.

556
00:36:00,389 --> 00:36:02,266
It was an accident.

557
00:36:03,351 --> 00:36:05,728
We were up in Carrington
for the retreat.

558
00:36:06,604 --> 00:36:10,024
Dilmer, Phippsy, Lem, and I
were out in the...the woods.

559
00:36:10,650 --> 00:36:14,946
You know, drinking a little,
shooting a little, blowing off steam.

560
00:36:15,113 --> 00:36:19,575
- Did Lem go shooting with you?
- No. Said he used enough guns.

561
00:36:20,451 --> 00:36:22,829
He drank up more than
his share of beers, though.

562
00:36:31,003 --> 00:36:32,046
Then Bosh...

563
00:36:32,171 --> 00:36:35,049
Sees this tree branch move
about 50 yards away.

564
00:36:35,800 --> 00:36:39,095
Thinks it's a deer.
And he starts shooting.

565
00:36:40,555 --> 00:36:42,598
- Hey, shh, shh!
- I know!

566
00:36:44,058 --> 00:36:45,101
Get down!

567
00:36:45,226 --> 00:36:46,644
"And we all start shooting."

568
00:36:58,447 --> 00:36:59,490
Whoo!

569
00:36:59,615 --> 00:37:00,992
- Yeah, baby!
- Whoo!

570
00:37:01,242 --> 00:37:02,910
- All right!
- It's mine!

571
00:37:03,035 --> 00:37:04,912
- I got me a deer!
- I hit it!

572
00:37:05,037 --> 00:37:06,289
Whoo-hoo!

573
00:37:06,455 --> 00:37:08,040
"We go to see what we got."

574
00:37:10,918 --> 00:37:12,086
"It was a man."

575
00:37:12,670 --> 00:37:14,338
"Everyone started
freaking out..."

576
00:37:17,091 --> 00:37:21,304
Lem wanted to go to the police.
It was an accident, he reasoned.

577
00:37:21,512 --> 00:37:26,684
But I know that wasn't a real option.
I'm a lawyer. I know what happens.

578
00:37:26,809 --> 00:37:31,063
We'd be in for years of legal hell
millions in lawyers' fees.

579
00:37:31,189 --> 00:37:34,108
- So Lem agreed to hide the body.
- We all agreed.

580
00:37:34,233 --> 00:37:36,611
- As a team.
- As a family.

581
00:37:38,154 --> 00:37:40,281
It was the best thing
for all of us.

582
00:37:40,865 --> 00:37:42,742
But when we got back,
Lem pulls me aside.

583
00:37:42,867 --> 00:37:44,118
He says he can't go through with it

584
00:37:44,243 --> 00:37:46,162
and he tries to convince me
that we can salvage this thing

585
00:37:46,287 --> 00:37:47,788
if we all just come clean.

586
00:37:48,748 --> 00:37:51,292
He was gonna confess
to the cops.

587
00:37:54,295 --> 00:37:55,463
So I agreed with him.

588
00:37:56,339 --> 00:37:58,758
Told him I needed a little bit of time
to get some things in order.

589
00:37:59,759 --> 00:38:02,428
But instead, you planned how
you were gonna get rid of Lem.

590
00:38:02,929 --> 00:38:05,139
Yes. That night.

591
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:09,936
"I asked him to open Catalina II"

592
00:38:10,228 --> 00:38:12,313
"to meet with some
late arriving clients."

593
00:38:14,899 --> 00:38:17,401
"It was scud duty
for the junior salesmen"

594
00:38:17,526 --> 00:38:19,070
"so he didn't think anything of it."

595
00:38:21,614 --> 00:38:23,574
"We did it together."

596
00:38:25,368 --> 00:38:27,245
"So that we'd all
be equally guilty."

597
00:38:30,122 --> 00:38:31,666
We were gonna dump him
and the car

598
00:38:31,791 --> 00:38:34,085
but when we got back
out of the house

599
00:38:34,210 --> 00:38:38,589
- the car w... it was gone.
- That's what screwed us.

600
00:38:40,549 --> 00:38:42,176
I still don't understand
what happened.

601
00:38:44,887 --> 00:38:46,138
Who took the car?

602
00:38:49,809 --> 00:38:52,645
"His car was parked out in front
of them fake houses so I took it."

603
00:38:54,146 --> 00:38:55,356
Where'd you guys get it?

604
00:38:59,318 --> 00:39:00,695
You can read about it
in the paper.

605
00:39:16,043 --> 00:39:18,796
Nice digs down here
in Major Crimes.

606
00:39:19,171 --> 00:39:20,256
Agent Wade.

607
00:39:20,965 --> 00:39:22,300
Hey, this is my partner
Wayne Rigsby.

608
00:39:22,425 --> 00:39:23,634
Rigsby, this is Tamsin Wade.

609
00:39:23,759 --> 00:39:25,720
She's heading the new
rapid response team for CBI.

610
00:39:25,845 --> 00:39:27,805
Oh, nice.
It's a pleasure to meet you.

611
00:39:27,972 --> 00:39:30,725
Likewise. That's actually why
I came to see you, Cho.

612
00:39:31,809 --> 00:39:32,852
What's up?

613
00:39:32,977 --> 00:39:35,313
I haven't selected an agent
from Major Crimes for the team.

614
00:39:35,479 --> 00:39:37,064
It wouldn't interfere
with your daily duties

615
00:39:37,189 --> 00:39:38,941
and I cleared it
with Agent Lisbon.

616
00:39:39,066 --> 00:39:41,902
So if you're interested,
we'd love to have you on board.

617
00:39:42,069 --> 00:39:43,988
- Sure.
- Great.

618
00:39:45,114 --> 00:39:49,160
Oh, and, uh, end of shift
a few of us are hitting the range

619
00:39:49,285 --> 00:39:51,954
if you want to come join us,
say hi to the team.

620
00:39:52,121 --> 00:39:53,289
I'll see you out there.

621
00:39:53,873 --> 00:39:55,541
- Agent Rigsby.
- Yeah.

622
00:40:01,005 --> 00:40:03,507
Lem's killer has led
the police up in Carrington

623
00:40:03,632 --> 00:40:05,343
to the body of the hunter
they killed.

624
00:40:05,760 --> 00:40:08,304
- Poor guy.
- Teresa, hey.

625
00:40:08,471 --> 00:40:10,723
Hey, Sarah. Thanks for coming by.

626
00:40:12,183 --> 00:40:13,726
I wanted you to meet Miss McVie

627
00:40:13,893 --> 00:40:16,729
before we made a decision
about the charges against her.

628
00:40:17,188 --> 00:40:19,648
This is Juliana
and her brother Noah.

629
00:40:20,649 --> 00:40:22,276
- Hi.
- Hey.

630
00:40:22,860 --> 00:40:25,237
Sarah works at the D.A.'s office.

631
00:40:25,905 --> 00:40:27,698
She just had a baby boy
last year.

632
00:40:28,491 --> 00:40:31,243
Yes, I did.
Lisbon, do you want to...

633
00:40:31,369 --> 00:40:33,829
And I was gonna tell them
that their brother died

634
00:40:33,954 --> 00:40:36,123
because he was going
to do the right thing.

635
00:40:36,916 --> 00:40:38,125
He's kind of a hero.

636
00:40:39,335 --> 00:40:42,296
Well, yeah, I guess
you could say that.

637
00:40:42,421 --> 00:40:46,133
And I was gonna say that given
the extenuating circumstances

638
00:40:46,258 --> 00:40:48,511
that maybe the CBI
and the D.A.'s office

639
00:40:48,636 --> 00:40:51,263
would consider dropping
the charges against Juliana.

640
00:40:53,015 --> 00:40:54,475
Because it's the right
thing to do.

641
00:41:01,732 --> 00:41:03,609
Yes. Okay.

642
00:41:05,528 --> 00:41:07,988
Nicely played, Teresa.
You owe me one.

643
00:41:08,739 --> 00:41:11,200
Good to meet you,
Juliana, Noah.

644
00:41:17,623 --> 00:41:22,211
- Charges dropped.
- I see what you did there.

645
00:41:23,045 --> 00:41:26,215
- Thank you. I-I really appreciate it.
- No problem.

646
00:41:28,467 --> 00:41:29,760
Oh. Wait.

647
00:41:35,266 --> 00:41:36,517
These are yours now.

648
00:41:40,604 --> 00:41:41,689
Thank you.

649
00:41:51,282 --> 00:41:52,324
Thank you.

650
00:41:54,076 --> 00:41:55,119
You're welcome.
